[Music]  Enoch love Zoo is a surgeon in a  hospital in Malawi he works long and  hard to help the hundred-plus  patients in his care he often has to  improvise because medication and  instruments are in short supply Malawi  is one of the world's poorest countries  the city of Samba south of capital along  way has a relatively well-equipped  hospital doctor looked sue and his team  received aid from Germany but they still  can't always give their patients the  care they need  [Music]  unless your son Edwin etre para is  pregnant and dr. Lutz ooh and his  assistant are concerned she has a tumor  on one leg and needs surgery  yeah the risk is the Preakness will go  with surgery  so what can you do  we need to know what it is we did the  excision last last year this is a  recurrent tumor unfortunately they were  not able to pay for the histology so it  didn't matter what we're dealing with  looks like a sarcoma so we need the  history once we have confirmation is  cancer the best I think is to amputate  my leg communicating bad news to  patients is an all too frequent part of  dr. Lutz's routine he makes the rounds  of the wards twice a week he and his  assistant devotes special attention to  the more difficult cases every patient  they're able to release is another  success such as this woman who came in  with ulcerated abdominal muscles another  patient has just been admitted with a  suspected rectal tumor what kind of  problem do you have I have blood in my  stool when did that start  it started when I had the baby the  history is she gave birth in March we  were there any changes in bowel habits  prior to passing stool not before I was  pregnant do your intestines make any  noise before a bowel movement I had  diarrhea and sometimes constipation what  did you Mark how often do you have these  problems quite often is this your only  illness there's nothing else he also  asks about her HIV status which is  negative  dr. Lutz ooh never manages to see all  his patients in one day and the ward is  almost always overcrowded but he still  takes time for every case he can three  procedures are scheduled for the  operating room the next morning that  might easily involve three improvised  solutions if the required equipment is  missing the first patient is barely  three months old she has hydrocephaly a  genetic defect that occurs relatively  frequently in Malawi and involves an  accumulation of fluid in the brain the  assistants have all had basic medical  training and continue to learn on the  job the more experienced among them can  even operate on their own an operation  won't help this defect a tube will have  to be run from the brain case down to  the abdominal cavity that will drain the  fluid off straight into the abdominal  cavity and take the pressure off the  baby's head testing the children Caesar  have analysis not on a dissecting  that's it you only have this one okay do  you have a big plumber nice let's just  go to yours right  Caesar so we have just improvise  something this time all that was missing  were the right size scissors they  proceed yo into where we are happy so  you we have which one of with the  attendees okay now the next is this one  listen and we take the bow suction as  the only surgeon here dr. Lutz ooh  shoulders a lot of responsibility he  teaches the assistants as much as he can  there are precious few fully qualified  doctors here yeah mainly what we need is  more an aphasia because the anesthetist  i shortened number so we don't read over  it to the full capacity of this theatre  dr. Lutz ooh is confronted with  shortages of equipment and medicine on a  daily basis as you want to do your job  you always want to have something that  you need to do the job in handy but  sometimes it's so difficult you don't  have this I don't of that but sometimes  it's frustrating pay obviously slow very  low so it's also frustrating he earns  the equivalent of around 450 euros a  month an extremely low wage for a  qualified surgeon at lunchtime dr. Lu  Zhu has to pick his daughter up from  daycare before returning to the clinic  it's the only break he gets from the  hospital routine dr. Lu Zhu is the first  surgeon to complete his studies and  training entirely in Malawi  and he's the only consultant surgeon at  the Zumba Central Hospital  my interest was to be an engineer  but then they've just changed when  sometime around the same video yeah my  father's to have this book bought modern  ways to health that was me driving so I  just took it and started reading it so  the story's out see about cider II put  my attention when I got very interested  I said ah this is how you really can  help  yeah in 2016 at age 37 dr. Lutzer moved  his family to Zomba as a state employee  he had to go where he was assigned he  hopes to work in the capital a long way  someday although he likes his work here  Sambas hospital is one of the few  central state clinics in Malawi it  serves a population of nearly 4 million  people the most complicated cases are  sent here there is no charge for  treatment the lines outside the  admissions office just keep growing  while the nurses try to assign the  patients according to urgency there's  still no casualty Department an average  of 15 patients are admitted here every  day the mortality rate at the hospital  is high close to 20 percent for some of  the wards it's never easy for an igloo  zoo to take when nothing can be done to  help the patients sometimes the problems  are beyond you for example somebody  needs blood there is no blood has a  niche bureau for what dude lays in match  again rubbish so sometimes the system  fails you sometimes maybe you can do  something the way you can do something  that's where we try as much as possible  we perform new hospital facilities or  extension buildings are financed almost  exclusively by nongovernmental  organizations a small casualty  Department is finally being built with  funding from the samba hospital project  in Germany the Association has been  supporting the clinic for the last 15  years its chairman olaf ba does what he  can to help dr. Lu su the typical  history of - say by illness you think  it's malaria sometimes the mayor may be  positive sometimes not you treat malaria  you don't improve then you  of abdominal pains which gets worsened  with typical title the two men work  together to try to increase the quality  of care in the surgery unit Olaf Bach  comes to Zomba twice a year for six  weeks at a time and oversees the  transport of obsolete medical equipment  from Germany German doctors and students  also come to help with the hospital  Olaf Bach is head surgeon at the  university clinic in llena it's fine  bringing it I think it's okay here and I  just opened a little bit down here and  she should go for dressing tomorrow  that's good enough  tell me Danny good bus that's good  that's good enough alright move the  fingers ah cranberry won't be any hmm  it's been around 20 years since boss  first came to Malawi as a young doctor  back then he also worked for a time here  in Samba I would not be the person I am  without Malawi I became shaped as a  doctor as a surgeon here I founded my  academic career by malaria research here  so I would not be in the position I am  in Germany now financially and  professionally without so I own  something to discuss they helped us a  lot in terms of equipment and other  materials which we usually lack and they  also transferring skills to  which is very good and  I think we are benefiting a lot from  this cooperation and with it continuous  an igloo Tzu is aware of how much he and  his clinic in Samba need the aid from  abroad like his commitment its  indispensable to providing the patients  here the care they need and ensuring  they receive treatment with dignity  [Music]

